{
  "requirement": "Each bundled skill's description MUST state when to use it in terms of the user's task. It MAY name another bundled skill to use instead where an agent could confuse the two. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query `requirements.cli.skill_conventions/skills`.**\n- It returns exactly the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` files: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`.\n- A text search for `^name: ` finds front matter only in those five files. The `references/*.md` files, which have no front matter, are correctly left out.\n- The match snippet (front matter followed by a heading) shows what the query selects. The no_match snippet (a heading with no front matter) shows what it leaves out. I found no skill the query misses and no out-of-scope place it selects.\n\n**Obligations.** The requirement has three parts:\n- A description MUST say when to use the skill, in terms of the user's task.\n- A description MAY name another bundled skill to use instead.\n- Each skill recognized by its front matter is a site.\n\nThe first is checked by one test. The MAY imposes nothing to test. The third is covered by the site query.\n\n**Test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skill_descriptions_say_when_to_use_them]`.**\n- For every skill in `SKILLS`, it checks the front matter. `name` and `description` each appear exactly once. The name matches the skill, and the description is non-empty and at most 1024 characters.\n- For each of the five skills, it asserts that the description starts with a specific \"Use at/during/when …\" phrase. Each phrase names the user's task: starting work, designing or changing requirements, refreshing evidence, adopting Habeas, or moving code, tests or requirements. A description that says nothing about when to use the skill would fail this.\n- Any `habeas-*` word in a description must be a bundled skill, so a named \"use instead\" skill is checked to exist.\n- It asserts that the confusable pairs name the right alternative: `habeas-requirements` to `habeas-refactor` and `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-evidence` to `habeas-refactor`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` to `habeas-evidence`, and `habeas-refactor` to `habeas-requirements`.\n\nThe skill list is hard-coded in `const[SKILLS]`. The site query still covers any skill added later. That test limitation is not something the requirement's text names, so I don't count it as a gap.",
  "references": "4fbf9a3072386612109271b7cf55673834816c2318c1c11463d91326147378e5",
